<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Itaipu Dam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Itaipu Dam is a hydroelectric dam on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parana-river">Paraná River</a> on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, built jointly by the two countries under a treaty of 1973. Construction began in 1975 and the first turbines went into operation in 1984. One of the largest power plants in the world, it supplies most of Paraguay's electricity, and Paraguay sells much of its share of the power to Brazil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/itaipu-dam.jpg" alt="Itaipu Dam" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jonas de Carvalho · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Itaipu_geral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The plan for a power plant on the Paraná grew out of negotiations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> in the 1960s. The Iguaçu Act, signed on 22 July 1966 by the foreign ministers Juracy Magalhães and Raúl Sapena Pastor, declared a shared interest in studying the use of the river's water from the Salto de Sete Quedas to the Iguaçu River watershed. The Itaipu Treaty followed on 26 April 1973, and the body Itaipu Binacional was created on 17 May 1974 to manage construction, which began in January of the following year. The name comes from an island near the site, called in Guarani after the idea of a sounding stone.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> first contested the project. The dispute ended with the Tripartite Agreement of 19 October 1979, which set the permitted river levels and became the basis of later Argentine–Brazilian integration. The river was diverted on 14 October 1978 to dry the section of bed where the dam was built.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The dam is 7,235 metres long and 196 metres high, and is really four dams joined: an earth fill dam, a rock fill dam, a concrete buttress main dam and a concrete wing dam. The reservoir began to fill on 13 October 1982, and the first generating unit started on 5 May 1984. The last two of the twenty units, each of 700 MW, began work in September 2006 and March 2007, bringing the capacity to 14 GW.</p><p>Ten units generate at 50 Hz for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and ten at 60 Hz for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Paraguay's output exceeds its own demand, so most of it goes directly to the Brazilian side, and two 600 kV direct-current lines about 800 kilometres long carry the bulk of the power to south-eastern Brazil. Around forty thousand people worked on the construction, which shifted 50 million tonnes of earth and rock.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The project displaced about 10,000 families and flooded the Guaíra Falls, the waterfall largest by volume in the world; some 80 people died when a crowded bridge over the falls collapsed shortly before the reservoir was filled. The falls had separated two freshwater ecoregions, and after they vanished more than 30 fish species from below them spread above. In 1994 the American Society of Civil Engineers named Itaipu one of the seven modern wonders of the world. The composer Philip Glass wrote a symphonic cantata called Itaipu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Itaipu Dam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
